WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:21.360
historic

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.960
If there's one place in the world where reptiles still rule,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.600
it's here in the Galapagos Islands.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.360
The ancestors of the reptiles, the amphibians,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.520
had wet, permeable skins.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.280
As a consequence, they couldn't exist for long away from water.

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.200
Reptiles, however, like these marine iguanas,

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:44.120
are not so restrictive.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.760
They can survive in places where amphibians

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.120
would roast to death within minutes.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.200
Out on the scorching lava fields,

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:55.040
the iguanas lie unprotected in the ferocious equatorial sun.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.040
They can do so because of the major innovation

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.640
made by the first reptiles of all, the nature of their skin.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:06.160
It's not moist like a frog's, but tough, covered with scales,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.520
and, most crucial of all, it's practically watertight.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.800
This skin has enabled reptiles to colonise the hottest

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.680
and driest places on earth, and this is one of them,

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.280
the Namib Desert in southwestern Africa.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.080
The sand here gets so hot, it scorches the skin,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.680
and even the sole of a reptile's foot can get painfully burnt,

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.440
so when the sun is at its worst, this little lizard gets relief.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.840
The lizard gets relief by gymnastics.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:03.960
The scales of the reptile's skin are dead,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.320
horny outgrowths like our fingernails,

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.440
and the reptiles use them for all kinds of purposes.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.920
Clearly, no bird would want to eat the Australian thorny devil

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.520
with scales like these.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:20.920
Scales are used to protect the body against wear and tear.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:22.920
Reptile legs stick out at the side

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.440
rather than provide support from underneath.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.720
Although this shingleback drags its belly along the ground,

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.520
its tough, heavy scales prevent damage.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:38.640
The scales can be of different sizes.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.400
They're small, where the skin needs to be flexible,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.920
and large and robust, especially on the head,

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.920
to reinforce the scalp.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.720
In some lizards, the horny skin and scales

00:03:51.720 --> 00:04:12.240
are fashioned into very dramatic headgear.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.240
Sometimes the adornments are designed not just to protect,

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.240
but to scare enemies, as in the bearded dragon.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.640
The Australian-filled lizard, like most of them, is a bluffer.

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.640
Its great ruff is no more than its scaly skin

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.640
supported by bones from the throat.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:58.640
In fact, it's a relatively harmless creature

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.640
with no real offensive weapons,

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.640
and if its bluff doesn't work...

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:30.640
it can run for it.

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:32.640
The horned iguana from the West Indies

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.640
is one of the most heavily armoured of all lizards.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.640
Big, powerful, it's the rhinoceros of the reptile world.

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.640
There's one group of lizards which, unusually for reptiles,

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:49.640
is active mostly at night, the geckos.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:58.640
Some of their scales are the most complicated of all.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.640
Geckos can run up vertical walls, even panes of glass,

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.640
and the trick is done by scales on the soles of the feet.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:09.640
Each scale is branched and carries hundreds of microscopic hairs

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.640
invisible to the naked eye.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.640
The electron microscope shows that each ends in a cluster of tiny hooks

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.640
that enables the gecko to hang on to virtually anything.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.640
The reptile's skin is rich in pigment cells

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:30.640
which can provide marvellous disguises.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.320
The

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.320
gask and gecko is coloured exactly like the bark it always sits on.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:41.640
The chameleon can vary the shade of green in its skin

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:44.640
so that it becomes invisible among leaves.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:49.640
The earless dragon from Central Australia

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:52.640
is obnoxious enough when it's feeding.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:55.640
But when it's motionless,

00:06:55.640 --> 00:07:00.640
it's very difficult indeed to distinguish it from pebbles.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:15.640
For the biggest scales of all, we have to go back to the Galapagos Islands

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:20.640
and look for their most famous inhabitant, the giant tortoise.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.640
Its scales are supported from beneath by massive bony plates

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:27.640
so that the animal is as impregnable as a tank.

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.640
The mountain slopes where they live are bone dry for much of the year,

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.640
but their watertight skin enables them to keep their liquid demands to a minimum.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:44.640
But even so, they have to top up with water,

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:48.640
and this is one of the few water holes in the crater.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:52.640
These few are just wallowing in the mud,

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:55.640
but at this very moment a shower of rain has started,

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.640
and it'll fill little puddles here in the mud,

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.640
and very soon there will be tortoises from all over the crater

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.640
screaming down here to sip in those puddles.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.640
Rumbling

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:23.640
Since the reptiles were the first backboneed animals

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.640
to live completely away from water,

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:30.640
they must have been the first creatures to develop real thirsts.

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:34.640
And they have vast capacities.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:37.640
They can store several gallons of liquid in their bodies.

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.640
So when water is about, they make the best of it

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:42.640
and fill their reserve tanks.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:45.640
This may well be their last drink for months.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.640
The tortoises are an extremely ancient group.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.640
They appeared right at the dawn of the age of reptiles,

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:02.640
some 180 million years ago.

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.640
They saw the dinosaurs come and they saw them vanish,

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:10.640
and they themselves continued, as far as we can judge from their fossils,

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.640
almost unchanged right until today.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.640
Rumbling

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.640
Their armoured shell may be unwieldy,

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:36.640
but it's obviously a most successful defence.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:43.640
Inside it, nothing can reach them.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:53.640
Rumbling

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:00.640
After a night of rain, the pools are full of water.

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:11.640
They're also full of wallowing tortoises.

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:14.640
Why, we just don't know, although some say that it's a way

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:18.640
of keeping warm at night and cool by day.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:24.640
Temperature control is something that all reptiles must achieve,

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:28.640
and some on the Galapagos must cope with the scorching equatorial sun

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.640
without the benefit of fresh water.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:35.640
Rumbling

00:10:38.640 --> 00:10:42.640
Marine iguanas live down on the hot black lava rocks.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.640
Although their skin is marvellously watertight,

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.640
it's a very poor insulator.

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:50.640
That may seem to be a limitation in this heat,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.640
but they've turned it very much to their advantage.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.640
Rumbling

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:05.640
We tend to think of reptiles as sluggish, cold-blooded creatures,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:08.640
but that's a very mistaken view.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.640
Some of them, like these marine iguanas, for example,

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:15.640
can maintain a higher working body temperature than we ourselves.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:19.640
In fact, the misleading term cold-blooded simply means

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:23.640
that the animals can't generate their own body heat internally.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:28.640
Instead, they get it directly from the sun by sunbathing.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.640
And what's more, they can control the amount of heat

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.640
that they absorb to within very fine limits.

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:45.640
At the moment, it's early morning.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.640
The night has been relatively cold,

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.640
and the iguanas are out on the rocks

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:52.640
soaking up all the heat that the sun can provide.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.640
For until they're warm, their body chemistry won't produce

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:59.640
the power that they need to be active.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.640
But overheating can be as dangerous as chilling.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.640
The iguanas can't cool themselves by sweating,

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:18.640
for the reptile's skin, for all its qualities,

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:20.640
hasn't got any sweat glands.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:24.640
So when around midday the sun gets too warm for comfort,

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.640
they move down into clefts in the rocks

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.640
and hang there in cool shadow.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:41.640
By choosing their resting places with care,

00:12:41.640 --> 00:12:43.640
they can keep their body temperature

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:47.640
very close to 37 degrees centigrade at all times.

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:50.640
When they've reached their working temperature,

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.640
they're able to go for a swim.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:01.640
These reptiles feed on seaweed,

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:04.640
and some of it they get by diving.

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:07.640
But the sun-warmed iguanas have a problem.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.640
The sea is particularly cold here.

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.640
A current comes straight up from the Antarctic,

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.640
and it's easy to get chilled and talking.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:20.640
So they partially overcome the difficulty

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:24.640
by withdrawing the warm blood into the centre of their body.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:27.640
It simply delays the cooling process.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:33.640
The iguanas are very cold, and they're very cold.

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.640
However, the marine iguanas must not stay out too long.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.640
Should they become over-chilled,

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.640
they will lose their energy

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.640
and no longer have the strength to cling to the rocks

00:13:55.640 --> 00:13:57.640
and to move on.

00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:11.640
By mid-afternoon, they're all back on the sun-bathing rocks,

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.640
eager to get warmed up again.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:16.640
The iguanas are very cold, and they're very cold.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:24.640
To get warm really quickly,

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:27.640
you need to expose as much as possible of your surface

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.640
to the sun and to the warm rocks,

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:32.640
so the most recently-emerged iguanas slump out,

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.640
spread-eagled, just like exhausted human bathers

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.640
after a really cold swim.

00:14:37.640 --> 00:14:39.640
And it's vital for them to warm up,

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:43.640
because, in hot warmth, they are unable to digest their meals.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:52.640
This is where they will all congregate as the day cools

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:56.640
in order to collect the last rays of the sinking sun.

00:14:56.640 --> 00:15:00.640
One of the advantages of generating your own body heat internally,

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:02.640
as we and all mammals do,

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:04.640
is that when the sun goes down,

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.640
we can remain active and we can live in cold climates.

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:11.640
But the price we pay for those privileges is very high.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.640
Something like 80% of the energy in the food that we eat

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:17.640
goes to maintaining our body temperatures

00:15:17.640 --> 00:15:19.640
at around 38 degrees centigrade.

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:22.640
The reptile system, getting their heat directly from the sun,

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:24.640
is very much more economical.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:28.640
A reptile can survive on about 10% of the food

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.640
that a mammal of a similar size would require.

00:15:31.640 --> 00:15:34.640
And so, although they can't live in the Arctic,

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:38.640
they can survive on very low-calorie foods, like the seaweed

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:40.640
that these marine iguanas eat.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:43.640
And they can also live in great numbers

00:15:43.640 --> 00:16:08.640
in places where food is very scarce, such as deserts.

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:12.640
A chameleon, for example, can flourish in the most barren area

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:14.640
provided that every few days

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:29.640
it catches one reasonably sized insect.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.640
The chameleon's talent for changing the colour of its skin

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.640
serves not only to give it different disguises,

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.640
but to express its emotions.

00:16:37.640 --> 00:16:39.640
It goes black with rage

00:16:39.640 --> 00:16:42.640
and becomes particularly brightly coloured when it's courting,

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.640
as many reptiles do.

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:49.640
Anolis lizards display with extensible throat pouches,

00:16:49.640 --> 00:17:00.640
each species with its own particular colour.

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.640
Nodding reinforces the effect.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.640
It's like waving a flag.

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:10.640
This is the rare green iguana from Fiji.

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:13.640
