00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:45.640
By comparison, the female is plainer and not nearly so demonstrative.

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:48.640
The Galapagos iguanas also nod.

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:52.640
For them, as for others, the gesture serves a double purpose,

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:57.640
not only to impress the females, but to warn off rival males.

00:17:57.640 --> 00:18:00.640
Each species has its own rhythm.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:06.640
The land iguanas have a slightly different language.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:09.640
A nose-to-nose nodding session is enough to settle

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:38.640
a territorial dispute between these two males.

00:18:38.640 --> 00:19:06.640
Eventually, all these displays lead to the desired consummation.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:10.640
The reptiles were the first vertebrates for whom internal fertilisation

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:12.640
was essential.

00:19:12.640 --> 00:19:16.640
Their immediate ancestors, the amphibians, had no need for it.

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:21.640
They mated in water, and sperms shed from the body could swim to the eggs.

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:25.640
But out on dry land, the male reptiles had to find some other way

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:31.640
of ensuring that sperm met egg by placing it inside the female's body.

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:36.640
It sometimes seems that that process for these antique creatures

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:42.640
is still a very clumsy and laborious business.

00:19:42.640 --> 00:19:48.640
And in due course, that produces the second of the great reptilian innovations.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:53.640
Their waterproof skins had enabled them to live in the driest places on Earth,

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:56.640
but that would be of limited value if they had to retreat

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:01.640
in order to find open water in which to lay their eggs, as the amphibians have to do.

