which makes it pretty clear that the river current flowed this way.

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:46.640
This is a plate from a young stegosaurus, the one with two rows of blades across its back,

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:54.640
and this the tooth of a savage allosaurus.

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:58.640
Fourteen different species of dinosaur have been found in this quarry,

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:02.640
ranging from tiny little creatures no bigger than the size of a chicken

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:07.640
to real monsters like the animal to whom this enormous thigh bone belonged,

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:12.640
which is one of the biggest land-living animals the world has ever seen.

00:25:12.640 --> 00:25:16.640
Most of the bones left in this quarry today come from carcasses

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:21.640
that have been dismembered either by the river or by scavenging reptiles.

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:24.640
But by the time quarrying finished here in the 1920s,

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:28.640
over 30 near-complete skeletons have been taken away,

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:34.640
and indeed many of the most beautiful and impressive dinosaur skeletons in our museums today

00:25:34.640 --> 00:25:41.640
come from this quarry or from other quarries working in the same formation.

00:25:41.640 --> 00:25:49.640
A brontosaurus, one of the biggest of all, over 60 feet long and in weighing in life about 30 tons,

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:53.640
that's to say about the same as three full-grown bull elephants.

00:25:53.640 --> 00:26:00.640
And the question immediately arises, why did these vegetable-eating dinosaurs grow to such a gigantic size?

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:03.640
Well, there are at least two possible answers.

00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:09.640
The first concerns their food, which was cycads and ferns.

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:15.640
Those sort of plants are extremely tough and fibrous and take a great deal of digestion.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:20.640
The dinosaurs only had relatively feeble teeth, which weren't much good at mastication,

00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:26.640
and therefore they had to have huge stomachs, which would serve as fermentation fats

00:26:26.640 --> 00:26:31.640
in which the food could be kept for very long periods of time while it was digested.

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:36.640
And a huge stomach requires a huge body to carry it.

00:26:36.640 --> 00:26:43.640
The second reason concerns that recurring problem for all reptiles, temperature control.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:52.640
The bigger your body, the less susceptible it is to variations in temperature because it retains its heat longer.

00:26:52.640 --> 00:27:01.640
And temperature control, too, may be the reason for the bizarre body of another famous dinosaur, the stegosaurus.

00:27:01.640 --> 00:27:05.640
It used to be said that these plates along its back were a kind of armour,

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:11.640
but close examination of them has shown that in life they were covered with a skin thick with blood,

00:27:11.640 --> 00:27:19.640
so that if the animal were broadside on to the sun, they would serve as solar panels, rapidly warming the blood.

00:27:19.640 --> 00:27:26.640
And if it were overheated and faced into the sun, then equally they would serve as very efficient cooling radiators.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:30.640
Such an ability to influence temperature could have been invaluable.

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:48.640
Certainly, for a plant eater to be sluggish on a cool morning could have been disastrous.

00:27:48.640 --> 00:27:53.640
Down in Texas, the muds of an ancient estuary now turned to rock and forming the bed of a river

00:27:53.640 --> 00:28:00.640
preserve a vivid record of these creatures.

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:07.640
This is the footprint of a fresh-eating dinosaur, a hunter, with huge talons on its two feet.

00:28:07.640 --> 00:28:14.640
It stood on its two feet upright, about 10 or 12 feet tall, with its tail on the ground,

00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:20.640
which here has ploughed into the mud, throwing up this great furrow.

00:28:20.640 --> 00:28:23.640
Here are two more of them.

00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:30.640
From their depth, we can get an idea of the animal's great weight.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:39.640
The line of tracks continues across the rock of the riverbed.

00:28:39.640 --> 00:28:44.640
And a little further down the river are the tracks of the reptile it may well have been stalking,

00:28:44.640 --> 00:28:52.640
one of those huge vegetarians, with footprints a yard across.

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:58.640
Farther north, in the Badlands of Montana, the muds and sands over which the dinosaurs roamed

00:28:58.640 --> 00:29:08.640
form cliffs of crumbling rocks.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:16.640
And here, weathering out near the top of this cliff, is the skull of one of the most dramatic

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:19.640
of all dinosaurs, Triceratops.

00:29:19.640 --> 00:29:20.640
It's a three-horned dinosaur.

00:29:20.640 --> 00:29:22.640
Here's one horn.

00:29:22.640 --> 00:29:25.640
Here's the second horn here, which has already weathered away.

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:32.640
And its nose is pointing that way so that its third horn on the tip of the nose is still in this rock here.

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:34.640
Here is its eye.

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:38.640
It has this great bony frill extending over its neck.

00:29:38.640 --> 00:29:45.640
In life, it was an immense creature, weighing perhaps 8 to 10 tons, 20 or 30 feet long.

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:54.640
It was a vegetarian, champing up with powerful scissor-like jaws, the cycads that grew in this neighborhood.

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:59.640
And this head alone weighed about two tons.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:06.640
The bony frill, while doubtless it served to protect the neck, also carried a great band of muscles here,

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:11.640
helping to manipulate and move this heavy head.

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:13.640
Its brain was comparatively large.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:18.640
Indeed, it was one of the largest of all brains of dinosaurs, weighing about two pounds.

00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:20.640
But that didn't save it.

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:26.640
This beast was one of the last of its kind.

00:30:26.640 --> 00:30:32.640
On a geological time scale, the disappearance of the dinosaurs was extraordinarily abrupt.

00:30:32.640 --> 00:30:36.640
And it's marked in the most dramatic way in these cliffs.

00:30:36.640 --> 00:30:43.640
Among the yellow and red sandstones and clays, there is this thin layer of coal along which I'm walking.

00:30:43.640 --> 00:30:48.640
Its black line rules an end to the reign of the dinosaurs.

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:52.640
In the beds immediately beneath it, there have been found in this area

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:57.640
the remains of at least nine or ten different species of dinosaur.

00:30:57.640 --> 00:31:00.640
Above it, there are none.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:05.640
Even though the end may have taken tens of thousands of years to be complete,

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:10.640
it was nonetheless extraordinarily abrupt and wholesale.

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:15.640
What on earth could have brought it about?

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:18.640
There have been dozens of suggestions.

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:22.640
The extreme require some kind of global catastrophe.

00:31:22.640 --> 00:31:25.640
But they're unlikely to be correct because after all,

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:29.640
it was only the dinosaurs that disappeared and not the whole of the reptiles.

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:34.640
A more reasonable idea is that it was the rise of the warm-blooded furry mammals

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:38.640
that caused the disappearance of the dinosaurs.

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:45.640
We can test the likelihood of that theory by having a look at the anthills that there are around here.

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:51.640
The ants in this part of the world roof their nests with gravel.

00:31:51.640 --> 00:31:59.640
And amongst the little chips of stone that they so laboriously haul here are things like... like this.

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:06.640
This is the tooth of a tiny mammal, a small shrew-like creature.

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:13.640
And that was the largest mammal that existed in this dinosaur-dominated part of the world.

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:20.640
It's inconceivable that such tiny creatures could have offered any real direct competition with the dinosaurs.

00:32:20.640 --> 00:32:25.640
No, there are better answers to the problem than that.

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:30.640
MUSIC

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:44.640
The America across which the dinosaurs roamed was covered with thick, moist jungle.

00:32:44.640 --> 00:32:52.640
But this fossilized tree, which was alive just after the last dinosaurs disappeared, is not of a jungle tree.

00:32:52.640 --> 00:32:59.640
This is a redwood, a sequoia, a tree which now and almost certainly then preferred a cool climate.

00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:06.640
And it's just one piece of a great body of evidence we have which goes to show that about 63 million years ago,

00:33:06.640 --> 00:33:13.640
almost simultaneous with the disappearance of the dinosaurs, the world went through a great change of climate.

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:15.640
It got colder.

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:21.640
In a cold climate, the absence of a good insulating skin could be lethal.

00:33:21.640 --> 00:33:25.640
While it's true that a big body retains heat for a long time,

00:33:25.640 --> 00:33:31.640
it's also true that such a body takes a very long time to regain it once it's been lost.

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:40.640
And so it could be that a succession of bitterly cold nights would be enough to drain a dinosaur of its heat beyond any recovery.

00:33:40.640 --> 00:33:49.640
And so such a cooling of the climate might, over hundreds and thousands of years, be enough to exterminate the entire race of dinosaurs.

00:33:49.640 --> 00:33:55.640
But of course the effect of very cold nights is much less on animals that spend their time in the water.

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:59.640
Water retains heat much longer than the air.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:20.640
And indeed there are giant water-living reptiles that have survived from the age of the dinosaur right until today.

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:44.640
This is a truly primeval scene.

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:51.640
Crocodiles have been lazing around swamps like this unchanged since the beginning of the age of the dinosaurs.

00:34:51.640 --> 00:35:11.640
At seven meters long and weighing three-quarters of a ton, a bull-nile crocodile is the biggest reptile alive today.

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:20.640
It's sometimes thought that reptiles in general, and dinosaurs in particular, were dull, stupid creatures with only the glimmerings of intelligence

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:22.640
and the simplest of behavior patterns.

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:29.640
That's a very mistaken view, as recent discoveries about the behavior of crocodiles have shown.

00:35:29.640 --> 00:35:33.640
Since water holds the heat, crocodiles spend the cool nights in the river.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:36.640
To them, it's like a warm bath.

00:35:36.640 --> 00:36:00.640
But as the sun rises, they emerge to boost their body temperatures in the sun.

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:06.640
To prevent themselves from overheating, they use one trick that marine iguanas don't have.

00:36:06.640 --> 00:36:26.640
They gape.

00:36:26.640 --> 00:36:32.640
Their mouth is lined with a much thinner skin than the heavily armored hide that covers their body,

00:36:32.640 --> 00:36:42.640
so the blood in the capillaries there is much more quickly cooled by the air.

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:56.640
And when, with the sun's help, their bodies are fully warmed up, they can move very fast indeed.

00:36:56.640 --> 00:37:00.640
They don't have lips, so their mouths are not water-tight.

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:04.640
If they were to swallow when they were submerged, water would flood down their throats.

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:09.640
So eating and swallowing has to be done above the surface.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:16.640
The surprising complexity of their family life may indicate how dinosaurs were capable of behaving.

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:19.640
Crocodiles mate in the water.

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:25.640
The 40 or so eggs are laid in a hole on the riverbank and covered with sand.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:31.640
Though the parents don't incubate the eggs, they usually remain close by.

00:37:31.640 --> 00:37:37.640
After about 90 days, when the eggs are about to hatch, the young, buried and still within their shells,

00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:45.640
begin calling, and the female starts to dig them up.

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:49.640
Then a very remarkable thing happens.

00:37:49.640 --> 00:38:11.640
At this time, the mother develops a pouch beneath her chin which will hold about seven eggs or young.

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:31.640
The eggs are about to hatch, as she knows from the chirps of the young inside them.

00:38:31.640 --> 00:38:59.640
She's taking them off to a special nursery area in another part of the swamp where there's better cover than beside the sandy bank where she nests.

00:38:59.640 --> 00:39:04.640
Burying eggs has its drawbacks. They can become damp and chilled.

00:39:04.640 --> 00:39:08.640
Nearby, there's another nest from which no young emerged.

00:39:08.640 --> 00:39:12.640
When the parents weren't looking, a predator dug them up and ate them.

00:39:12.640 --> 00:39:25.640
Now the remains are only food for ants and beetles.

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:31.640
This is the culprit, a monitor lizard, and it will take babies just as eagerly as eggs.

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:43.640
With predators like this around, it pays crocodiles to guard their clutch.

00:39:43.640 --> 00:40:05.640
The mother returns in the nick of time.

00:40:13.640 --> 00:40:37.640
It's tempting to think that the great dinosaurs may have cared just as delicately for their babies.

00:40:37.640 --> 00:41:02.640
Even the bull responds to the sight and sounds of the young.

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:20.640
Young is still in the zone today.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:59.160
Like all reptile hatchlings, the young are miniature versions of the adults and capable

00:41:59.160 --> 00:42:26.160
of finding food for themselves from the moment that they leave the shell.

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:44.440
The babies themselves could be a meal for birds or other crocodiles, so the parents

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:51.760
watch over them while they perfect their hunting skills.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:56.200
Animals together with tortoises have changed little over the past ages.

00:42:56.200 --> 00:43:01.480
The ancestral reptiles were walkers, but the most sophisticated of all modern forms have

00:43:01.480 --> 00:43:17.580
completely changed their style of getting around.

00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:19.620
It all started with lizards.

00:43:19.620 --> 00:43:24.400
They too are an ancient group, but early in their history they gave rise to a particularly

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:27.000
important and successful family.

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:30.760
The lizards for some reason have a tendency to lose their legs.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:35.800
Some are still in the process of doing so today and live under rocks or burrow where

00:43:35.800 --> 00:43:38.080
legs could get in the way.

00:43:38.080 --> 00:43:43.220
The Australian blue tongue skink has only very small legs.

00:43:43.220 --> 00:43:48.800
In this South African skink they've virtually disappeared.

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:57.080
And it moves by wriggling.

00:43:57.080 --> 00:44:03.200
The Australian scaly foot, as its name suggests, has only a pair of stumps at the rear to betray

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:11.240
the fact that it's a burrowing lizard.

00:44:11.240 --> 00:44:15.720
This grotesque creature has all but lost its eyes as well as its legs.

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:22.760
It's an amphis beanid and normally lives entirely underground.

00:44:22.760 --> 00:44:27.280
Some hundred million years ago another group of lizards also took to burrowing and they

00:44:27.280 --> 00:44:29.080
lost their legs.

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:34.280
Some of their descendants came back above ground and became snakes.

00:44:34.280 --> 00:44:39.540
Pythons and boas still retain evidence that they once had limbs, two tiny spurs where

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:42.160
their hind legs once were.

00:44:42.160 --> 00:44:46.840
Without legs the snakes had to develop new techniques for getting around and very efficient

00:44:46.840 --> 00:44:50.860
they are too.

00:44:50.860 --> 00:44:56.640
The boa's regular method is to throw its body into S-shaped coils so that its flanks get

00:44:56.640 --> 00:45:00.840
purchase on irregularities on the surface and the rough scales underneath get a grip

00:45:00.840 --> 00:45:09.080
on the ground so that as the coils move backwards the snake is able to thrust itself forwards.

00:45:09.080 --> 00:45:13.080
Snakes like most snakes can also move in a straight line by shuffling along on their

00:45:13.080 --> 00:45:19.120
ribs and that's very useful for crawling along a branch.

00:45:19.120 --> 00:45:24.480
The puff adder, when it's stalking its prey, also moves on its ribs lifting them in groups

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:30.000
and pulling the scales of its underside forward and over the rib tips so that undulations

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.440
pass down the lower half of the body.

00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:38.560
To me however the most mystifying technique is that used by the sidewinder in the deserts

00:45:38.560 --> 00:45:39.560
of South West Africa.

00:46:08.560 --> 00:46:23.240
The key to understanding how it works is to watch for the only two places where at any

00:46:23.240 --> 00:46:26.240
one time its body touches the ground.

00:46:26.240 --> 00:46:33.440
It is in effect taking a series of steps sideways and with only two points of contact the hot

00:46:33.440 --> 00:46:39.240
sand doesn't burn it.

00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:43.920
With such methods of stalking and chasing their prey the snakes have become formidable

00:46:43.920 --> 00:46:48.680
hunters.

00:46:48.680 --> 00:46:54.760
Many snakes have become swimmers and hunt underwater.

00:46:54.760 --> 00:47:00.160
This venomous Florida mud snake has caught a siren, a sort of amphibian.

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:04.880
Some are prepared to catch the most spiky of meals and subdue them by throwing coils

00:47:04.880 --> 00:47:09.800
around them.

00:47:09.800 --> 00:47:12.960
This might seem an impossible mouthful.

00:47:12.960 --> 00:47:17.400
Most snakes have jaws that are specially hinged to give them a great gape but that of the

00:47:17.400 --> 00:47:30.920
egg eating snake is simply vast.

00:47:30.920 --> 00:47:57.080
Once swallowed it cracks the egg by grinding it against spikes that project into the gut

00:47:57.080 --> 00:48:04.200
from beneath the spine.

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:07.680
In many parts of the world snakes flourish in huge numbers.

00:48:07.680 --> 00:48:12.680
They are so unobtrusive that it is difficult to appreciate just how many there are except

00:48:12.680 --> 00:48:14.960
on special occasions.

00:48:14.960 --> 00:48:18.600
One such occurs every spring in southern Canada.

00:48:18.600 --> 00:48:25.440
Prairie garter snakes hibernate commonly and when spring comes a flood of newly warm snakes

00:48:25.440 --> 00:48:28.840
spills from the limestone pits where they have wintered.

00:48:28.840 --> 00:48:35.480
As soon as they emerge they mate and in the most spectacular fashion.

00:48:35.480 --> 00:48:40.800
Each female leaves a trail of sexual scent which excites and attracts up to a hundred

00:48:40.800 --> 00:49:04.680
of the smaller males which frantically struggle to become her mate.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:29.480
These garter snakes are very advanced members of their group.

00:49:29.480 --> 00:49:33.040
Not only have they developed the technique of hibernation in order to live through the

00:49:33.040 --> 00:49:37.800
winters when the ground is covered with deep snow but they have also managed to overcome

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:41.280
many of the limitations of egg laying.

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:58.440
A few months after this communal mating the offspring appear.

00:49:58.440 --> 00:50:03.640
Instead of laying and abandoning her eggs as most snakes do the female garter snake

00:50:03.640 --> 00:50:06.060
becomes a mobile incubator.

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:11.560
She retains her batch of eggs inside her body thereby protecting them and by basking in

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:14.500
the sun she keeps them warm.

00:50:14.500 --> 00:50:18.900
She even contributes to the nourishment of the embryos over the course of three months

00:50:18.900 --> 00:50:46.560
almost like mammals do until the time comes for them to be born.

00:50:46.560 --> 00:51:02.520
Livebearing is practiced by several snakes including some that have a strong claim to

00:51:02.520 --> 00:51:05.920
be the most highly evolved reptiles of all.

00:51:05.920 --> 00:51:14.840
You can find them in the deserts of the western United States.

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:19.280
Garter snakes this is the western diamondback.

00:51:19.280 --> 00:51:25.960
No animal alive can excel these creatures when it comes to finding stalking and dispatching

00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:33.520
their victims.

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:36.720
Its scales serve it in several ways.

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:47.560
Those on its flanks are grooved to increase their efficiency as heat absorbers.

00:51:47.560 --> 00:51:58.160
Those on its tail are hollow rattles.

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:03.440
A pit beneath its eye is so sensitive to heat that it can detect the body warmth of a small

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:09.000
mammal as much as half a meter away.

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:12.360
The snakes flickering tongue tastes the air.

00:52:12.360 --> 00:52:20.120
This is a Mexican blacktail rattlesnake.

00:52:20.120 --> 00:52:25.300
It collects the smell as molecules in the air and then carries them back to a sensory

00:52:25.300 --> 00:52:37.480
pit in the top of its mouth for tasting.

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:41.680
And when it's found its prey it strikes.

00:52:41.680 --> 00:52:46.920
The huge poison fangs hinge forward ready to inject one of the most lethal poisons in

00:52:46.920 --> 00:53:02.600
the world.

00:53:02.600 --> 00:53:10.320
A rattlesnake can survive here on only a dozen or so meals in a year and that's pretty efficient.

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:15.960
But even so being cold-blooded solar powered does have its limitations.

00:53:15.960 --> 00:53:23.240
No reptile can survive sustained cold and so great areas of the world are close to them.

00:53:23.240 --> 00:53:28.400
But a very long time ago one group of the reptiles evolved an answer to that problem

00:53:28.400 --> 00:53:29.400
too.

00:53:29.400 --> 00:53:35.440
An answer that was based once again on that very versatile thing the reptilian scale.

00:53:35.440 --> 00:53:46.960
A feather.

